1. Finds the `.sidebar` element and reads its bounding rectangle to get its width.
2. Finds the `#view-setup` element and reads its bounding rectangle to get its `x` position (offset from the left edge) and its width.
3. Checks the computed `display` property of `.nav-label` to see if the labels are visible (e.g., `"none"` vs `"block"`).
4. Returns all these values in a JSON string so the tool can easily parse it.

I'll wrap this logic in an IIFE (immediately invoked function expression) to keep the expression self‑contained. The code will look like:

```js
(() => {
  const sb = document.querySelector('.sidebar').getBoundingClientRect();
  const view = document.querySelector('#view-setup').getBoundingClientRect();
  return JSON.stringify({
    sidebar_w: sb.width,
    view_x: Math.round(view.x),
    view_w: Math.round(view.width),
    labels_visible: getComputedStyle(document.querySelector('.nav-label')).display
  });
})()
```

